= 1E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= Solution
{parent=1E}

For a <permutation> $\sigma\in S_n$, let $P_\sigma$ be its <permutation matrix>, defined by $P_\sigma e_j=e_{\sigma(j)}$. Define the <signature of a permutation> by $\epsilon(\sigma)=\det P_\sigma$. A <permutation matrix> is orthogonal, so $(\det P_\sigma)^2=1$ and its <permutation sign> lies in $\{1,-1\}$. A <transposition> exchanges two columns of the identity and therefore has <determinant> $-1$. Every <permutation> is a product of <transpositions>, by decomposing each <permutation cycle> as
$$
(a_1\,a_2\,\ldots,a_r)=(a_1\,a_r)(a_1\,a_{r-1})\cdots(a_1\,a_2).
$$
Hence $\epsilon(\sigma)=(-1)^m$ for any expression as $m$ <transpositions>; the <determinant> definition proves that this <parity of a permutation> is independent of the expression. Equivalently the <permutation sign> is positive for an <even permutation> and negative for an <odd permutation>.

Composition is taken rightmost first. Since $P_{\sigma\tau}e_j=e_{\sigma(\tau(j))}=P_\sigma P_\tau e_j$, we have $P_{\sigma\tau}=P_\sigma P_\tau$. Multiplicativity of the <determinant> therefore proves the <sign homomorphism> identity
$$
\boxed{\epsilon(\sigma\tau)=\epsilon(\sigma)\epsilon(\tau).}
$$
The <alternating group> consists of the <even permutations>:
$$
\boxed{A_n=\{\sigma\in S_n:\epsilon(\sigma)=1\}=\ker\epsilon.}
$$
It contains the identity. If $\sigma,\tau\in A_n$, their product has <permutation sign> $1$, and $\epsilon(\sigma^{-1})=\epsilon(\sigma)^{-1}=1$, so their inverses also belong to $A_n$. This proves it is a <subgroup>. Finally, for every $g\in S_n$ and $a\in A_n$,
$$
\epsilon(gag^{-1})=\epsilon(g)\epsilon(a)\epsilon(g)^{-1}=1.
$$
Thus **$A_n$ is a <normal subgroup> of $S_n$**. This also exhibits directly why a <kernel of a group homomorphism> is normal.

= 2E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= Solution
{parent=2E}

The real <orthogonal group> and <special orthogonal group> are, under <matrix> multiplication,
$$
\boxed{O(n)=\{Q\in M_n(\mathbb R):Q^TQ=I\},\qquad
SO(n)=\{Q\in O(n):\det Q=1\}.}
$$
An <orthogonal matrix> preserves Euclidean <inner products>; taking <determinants> of $Q^TQ=I$ shows that its <determinant> is $1$ or $-1$. Its inverse is $Q^T$, so $QQ^T=I$ as well.

For $Q\in SO(3)$, use $Q-I=Q(I-Q^T)$. The <determinant> then satisfies
$$
\det(Q-I)=\det Q\det(I-Q^T)=\det(I-Q)=(-1)^3\det(Q-I).
$$
It follows that $\det(Q-I)=0$. Thus the real <matrix> $Q-I$ has a nonzero <vector> in its <kernel of a linear map>, yielding
$$
\boxed{Qv=v\quad\text{for some }v\ne0.}
$$
This is the three-dimensional case of <odd-dimensional special orthogonal transformation has a fixed vector>; geometrically it supplies the fixed <rotation> axis, unless the transformation is the identity and every direction is fixed.

The claim is **false for $O(3)$**. The <matrix> $Q=-I_3$ is orthogonal with <determinant> $-1$ and has only the <eigenvalue> $-1$. It therefore has no nonzero <eigenvector> with <eigenvalue> $1$.

= 3C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= i
{parent=3c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Differentiate the parametrized <curve> to obtain $\boldsymbol x'(t)=(1-t^2,2t,1+t^2)$. Its <speed> obeys
$$
|\boldsymbol x'(t)|^2=(1-t^2)^2+4t^2+(1+t^2)^2=2(1+t^2)^2.
$$
Since $1+t^2>0$, the <arc length> is
$$
\boxed{L=\int_0^1\sqrt2(1+t^2)\,dt=\frac{4\sqrt2}{3}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=3c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Divide the velocity by the <speed> calculated above. The <unit tangent vector> is
$$
\boxed{T(t)=\frac1{\sqrt2}\left(\frac{1-t^2}{1+t^2},\frac{2t}{1+t^2},1\right).}
$$
Its derivative is
$$
T'(t)=\frac1{\sqrt2(1+t^2)^2}\bigl(-4t,2(1-t^2),0\bigr),
\qquad |T'(t)|=\frac{\sqrt2}{1+t^2}>0.
$$
Because $ds/dt=\sqrt2(1+t^2)>0$, the <principal normal vector> has the direction of $T'(t)$, giving
$$
\boxed{N(t)=\left(-\frac{2t}{1+t^2},\frac{1-t^2}{1+t^2},0\right).}
$$
This is a <unit vector>, and $N\cdot e_z=0$ for every real $t$. The <curvature of a space curve> is $|T'|/(ds/dt)=1/(1+t^2)^2>0$, so the <principal normal vector> is defined everywhere on this <curve>. The same perpendicularity follows from <constant tangent component forces perpendicular principal normals>: $T\cdot e_z=1/\sqrt2$ is constant, and differentiating this identity forces the normal component along $e_z$ to vanish.

= 4C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= Solution
{parent=4C}

For a constant orthogonal Cartesian coordinate change $x'_i=R_{ij}x_j$, a <Cartesian second-rank tensor> has components satisfying
$$
\boxed{T'_{ij}(x')=R_{ik}R_{jl}T_{kl}(x).}
$$
Both indices transform by the coordinate-change <matrix>; summation over repeated indices is understood by the <Einstein summation convention>. A <vector> would have the one-index rule $U'_i=R_{ik}U_k$.

Define $U_i=\partial T_{ij}/\partial x_j$. Since $x_m=R_{jm}x'_j$, the <chain rule> gives $\partial/\partial x'_j=R_{jm}\partial/\partial x_m$. The <rotation> coefficients are constant, so
$$
\begin{aligned}
U'_i&=\frac{\partial T'_{ij}}{\partial x'_j}
=R_{jm}R_{ik}R_{jl}\frac{\partial T_{kl}}{\partial x_m}\\
&=R_{ik}\delta_{ml}\frac{\partial T_{kl}}{\partial x_m}
=R_{ik}\frac{\partial T_{kl}}{\partial x_l}=R_{ik}U_k.
\end{aligned}
$$
The identity $R_{jm}R_{jl}=\delta_{ml}$ follows from orthogonality. Hence **the contracted derivative transforms as a <vector>**. This proves the <divergence of a Cartesian second-rank tensor> rule for global Cartesian <rotations>. In curvilinear coordinates the corresponding construction uses a <covariant derivative>; ordinary partial derivatives alone would not have the stated tensorial transformation law.

= 5E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= Solution
{parent=5E}

For a <normal subgroup> $H\trianglelefteq G$, define multiplication of left <cosets> by
$$
\boxed{(gH)(kH)=(gk)H.}
$$
To check that this is well-defined, replace $g,k$ by $gh_1,kh_2$ with $h_1,h_2\in H$. Their product is $gh_1kh_2=gk(k^{-1}h_1k)h_2$, and normality puts $(k^{-1}h_1k)h_2$ in $H$. Thus the product <coset> is independent of the representatives. <Associativity> follows from multiplication in $G$, the identity is $H$, and the inverse of $gH$ is $g^{-1}H$. These operations form the <quotient group> $G/H$.

For the unheaded finite-index conclusion, let $G$ act on the $n$ left <cosets> of its proper <subgroup> $H$ by $g\cdot(xH)=gxH$. This is well-defined even if $H$ is not normal: replacing $x$ by $xh$ leaves $gxH$ unchanged. The maps are bijections with inverses supplied by $g^{-1}$, and their compositions give a <group homomorphism> $\varphi:G\to S_n$. Its <kernel of a group homomorphism> $N$ is normal, by the calculation in part (ii).

The action is not trivial, because some $g\notin H$ sends the <coset> $H$ to $gH\ne H$; hence $N\ne G$. If $N$ were trivial, $\varphi$ would be <injective>, implying $|G|\leq|S_n|=n!$. Under the given strict inequality this is impossible. Thus $N$ is a nontrivial proper <normal subgroup>, and
$$
\boxed{|G|>n!\ \Longrightarrow\ G\text{ is not simple}.}
$$
More precisely, $N=\bigcap_{x\in G}xHx^{-1}$ is the <subgroup core>: $g$ fixes every <coset> if and only if $x^{-1}gx\in H$ for every $x$. This identifies the <normal subgroup> produced by the <coset action>.

= i
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume $H$ is a <normal subgroup>. Take the <quotient group> $K=G/H$ and the natural projection $\theta(g)=gH$. The well-defined multiplication constructed above gives $\theta(gk)=\theta(g)\theta(k)$, so this is a <group homomorphism>. Its identity is the <coset> $H$, and $\theta(g)=H$ exactly when $g\in H$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\ker\theta=H,}
$$
which proves the implication from normality to being the <kernel of a group homomorphism>.

= ii
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume $H=\ker\theta$ for a <group homomorphism> $\theta:G\to K$. For $h\in H$ and $g\in G$,
$$
\theta(ghg^{-1})=\theta(g)\theta(h)\theta(g)^{-1}=e_K.
$$
Thus $gHg^{-1}\subseteq H$. Apply the same inclusion with $g^{-1}$ to obtain the reverse inclusion, giving $gHg^{-1}=H$. Hence **every kernel is a <normal subgroup>**, proving the reverse implication and the required equivalence.

= 6E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= Solution
{parent=6E}

For any <permutation> $\tau$, conjugating a <permutation cycle> relabels its entries:
$$
\tau(a_1\,a_2\,\ldots,a_r)\tau^{-1}=(\tau(a_1)\,\tau(a_2)\,\ldots,\tau(a_r)).
$$
Indeed, the conjugate sends $\tau(a_j)$ to $\tau(a_{j+1})$ and fixes the other relabeled entries exactly as the original <permutation cycle> does. Therefore <conjugation> preserves the multiset of <permutation cycle> lengths. Conversely, if two <permutations> have the same <cycle type>, pair their <permutation cycles> of each length and let $\tau$ carry the $j$th entry of each first <permutation cycle> to the $j$th entry of its partner. Include one-cycles for fixed points. This defines a <bijection> of all labels, and the displayed relabeling identity makes the <permutations> conjugate. Thus
$$
\boxed{\sigma\sim_{S_n}\rho\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\sigma,\rho\text{ have the same cycle type}.}
$$
For $n\geq2$, the <alternating conjugacy class splitting criterion> says that an even <permutation> has the same class in $A_n$ and $S_n$ precisely when its type is not a collection of pairwise distinct odd lengths. Equivalently, **there is an even-length <permutation cycle> or a repeated <permutation cycle> length**, counting each fixed point as a length-one <permutation cycle>. Distinct odd lengths instead give two alternating-group classes. For $n=1$, $A_1=S_1$ and the sole class is the same; the index-two splitting criterion is not applicable.

Squaring an odd-length <permutation cycle> keeps one <permutation cycle> of that length, since advancing by two visits all of its entries. Squaring an even-length <permutation cycle> splits it into two <permutation cycles>, each of half that length. Thus any even-length <permutation cycle> strictly increases the total <permutation cycle> count on squaring, while odd <permutation cycles> leave that count unchanged. The <cycle type> can agree with its square exactly when every length is odd. This proves <permutation conjugate to its square>:
$$
\boxed{\sigma\sim_{S_n}\sigma^2\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\text{every cycle length is odd}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\operatorname{ord}(\sigma)\text{ is odd}.}
$$
The final equivalence follows because the <order of a group element> that is a <permutation> is the <least common multiple> of its <permutation cycle> lengths.

The possible even types on five labels are the identity, a three-cycle, a product of two disjoint <transpositions> and a five-cycle. The identity and the double <transpositions> are their own inverses. For a three-cycle $(a\,b\,c)$ with unused labels $d,e$, the even <permutation> $(b\,c)(d\,e)$ conjugates it to its inverse. For a five-cycle $(a\,b\,c\,d\,e)$, the even <permutation> $(b\,e)(c\,d)$ reverses its order. Therefore **every element of $A_5$ is conjugate to its inverse within $A_5$**. Inverting a five-cycle uses two <transpositions>, in agreement with <inversion of an odd cycle in an alternating group>.

A small counterexample elsewhere is
$$
\boxed{n=3,\qquad\sigma=(1\,2\,3).}
$$
The <alternating group> $A_3=\{e,(1\,2\,3),(1\,3\,2)\}$ is cyclic and therefore abelian. Every <conjugacy class> in an <abelian group> is a singleton, while $\sigma\ne\sigma^{-1}$. Thus these two elements are not conjugate in $A_3$.

= 7E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= Solution
{parent=7E}

Write a <Möbius transformation> as $M(z)=(az+b)/(cz+d)$ with $ad-bc\ne0$, interpreted on the <Riemann sphere>. Let $T_u(z)=z+u$, $D_\lambda(z)=\lambda z$ with $\lambda\ne0$, and $I(z)=1/z$.

If $c=0$, then $a,d\ne0$ and $M(z)=(a/d)z+b/d=T_{b/d}\circ D_{a/d}(z)$. If $c\ne0$, algebraic division gives
$$
M(z)=\frac ac+\frac{bc-ad}{c^2}\frac1{z+d/c}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{M=T_{a/c}\circ D_{(bc-ad)/c^2}\circ I\circ T_{d/c}.}
$$
The scaling coefficient is nonzero because $ad-bc\ne0$. Thus translations, nonzero complex scalings and reciprocal inversion generate every <Möbius map>. The formulas extend over their poles and infinity by the sphere interpretation.

= i
{parent=7e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The assertion is **true**. A <Möbius map> fixes infinity if and only if $c=0$: if $c\ne0$, its value there is the finite number $a/c$. For $c=0$, the decomposition above uses only a nonzero complex scaling and a translation. These maps form the <affine subgroup of the Möbius group>.

= ii
{parent=7e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The assertion is **false**. Let $D_\lambda(z)=\lambda z$ and $I(z)=1/z$. Their relations are
$$
I^2=\operatorname{id},\qquad ID_\lambda=D_{\lambda^{-1}}I.
$$
Moving each inversion to the right reduces every word to $D_\lambda I^\epsilon$ with $\epsilon=0$ or $1$. Thus the <subgroup generated by complex scalings and reciprocal inversion> consists only of $z\mapsto\lambda z$ and $z\mapsto\lambda/z$. Of these, only the first form fixes zero.

The <Möbius transformation>
$$
\boxed{M(z)=\frac z{z+1}}
$$
fixes zero but is not a scaling: it sends infinity to $1$ instead of infinity. It therefore cannot belong to the <subgroup> generated by scalings and reciprocal inversion.

= iii
{parent=7e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The assertion is **true** over the complex numbers. It suffices to generate every nonzero scaling using translations and $I(z)=1/z$, since these together with scalings already generate the <Möbius group>.

For $s\ne0$, compose the maps rightmost first. Away from the intermediate poles, the successive values are
$$
z\longmapsto\frac1z\longmapsto\frac{1+sz}{z}\longmapsto\frac z{1+sz}
\longmapsto-\frac1{s(1+sz)}\longmapsto-s(1+sz)\longmapsto-s^2z.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{T_s\circ I\circ T_{-1/s}\circ I\circ T_s\circ I=D_{-s^2}.}
$$
For every $\lambda\ne0$, choose a complex square root $s$ with $s^2=-\lambda$. This gives $D_\lambda$ and proves <Möbius scaling generated by translations and reciprocal inversion>. Equality of the rational maps extends the identity to all points of the <Riemann sphere>, including the intermediate exceptional values.

= 8E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= Solution
{parent=8E}

Let a finite <group> $G$ act on a set $X$. For $x\in X$, define its <stabilizer subgroup> $G_x=\{g:g\cdot x=x\}$ and its <group orbit> $Gx=\{g\cdot x:g\in G\}$. The map
$$
gG_x\longmapsto g\cdot x
$$
is well-defined, because multiplying $g$ on the right by an element fixing $x$ does not change its image. It is onto by the definition of the orbit. If $g\cdot x=h\cdot x$, then $h^{-1}g\in G_x$, so $gG_x=hG_x$; hence it is one-to-one. Every <coset> has $|G_x|$ elements and the <cosets> partition $G$. This proves the <orbit-stabilizer theorem>:
$$
\boxed{|G|=|Gx|\,|G_x|.}
$$
For a <group> element $x$, its <cyclic subgroup> $H=\langle x\rangle$ has $\operatorname{ord}(x)$ elements. Apply the theorem to the action of $G$ on its left <cosets> $G/H$, whose stabilizer at $H$ is $H$. It follows that
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}(x)=|H|\text{ divides }|G|.}
$$
This is the needed case of <Lagrange's theorem>, obtained directly from the action.

To prove <Cauchy's theorem for finite groups>, let $p$ be a prime divisor of $|G|$ and consider
$$
\mathcal X=\{(g_1,\ldots,g_p)\in G^p:g_1\cdots g_p=e\}.
$$
The first $p-1$ entries are arbitrary and determine the last, so $|\mathcal X|=|G|^{p-1}$ is divisible by $p$. Cyclically rotate the entries. This preserves the product constraint even if $G$ is nonabelian, since
$$
g_2\cdots g_pg_1=g_1^{-1}(g_1\cdots g_p)g_1=e.
$$
Thus a <cyclic group> of order $p$ acts on $\mathcal X$. By the <orbit-stabilizer theorem>, its orbits have size one or $p$. Its fixed tuples are exactly $(g,\ldots,g)$ with $g^p=e$. If $m$ is their number, counting the remaining size-$p$ orbits gives $m\equiv|\mathcal X|\equiv0\pmod p$. The identity provides one fixed tuple, so $m\geq p$ and a nonidentity $g$ with $g^p=e$ exists. The order of this $g$ divides $p$: division of $p$ by its order and minimality of that order prove this directly. Since $p$ is prime and $g\ne e$, the order is exactly $p$. This is the <cyclic-tuple proof of Cauchy theorem>.

Now suppose every nonidentity element has order two. Then every element equals its inverse, and for any $a,b\in G$,
$$
ab=(ab)^{-1}=b^{-1}a^{-1}=ba.
$$
Thus <group of exponent two is abelian> applies. Starting from the identity <subgroup>, choose an element outside the <subgroup> already generated. Because the <group> is abelian and that element squares to the identity, adjoining it doubles the <subgroup>: it is the disjoint union of the old <subgroup> and its translate by the new element. Repeating until the <finite group> is exhausted shows $|G|=2^r$. Conversely the <direct product of groups> $C_2^r$ has order $2^r$ and every nonidentity element has order two. Therefore
$$
\boxed{n=2^r\quad(r=0,1,2,\ldots).}
$$
The case $r=0$ is the trivial <group> and satisfies the condition vacuously. These finite examples are <elementary abelian groups> of exponent two.

An infinite example is the <group> of all binary sequences, with coordinatewise addition modulo two:
$$
\boxed{G=\prod_{j=1}^\infty\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z.}
$$
Every sequence added to itself is zero, so every nonzero sequence has order exactly two. The sequences having a single $1$ in coordinate $j$ are all distinct, proving that the <group> is infinite.

= 9C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= i
{parent=9c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <vector triple product> removes the nested <cross products>:
$$
F_i=\omega_i\omega_jx_j-|\boldsymbol\omega|^2x_i.
$$
Since $\boldsymbol\omega$ is constant, the <divergence> is
$$
\partial_iF_i=\omega_i\omega_i-3|\boldsymbol\omega|^2=-2|\boldsymbol\omega|^2.
$$
The cube has <volume> $2^3=8$. With the outward <unit normal>, the <divergence theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\int_S\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=-16|\boldsymbol\omega|^2.}
$$

= ii
{parent=9c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The derivative <matrix>
$$
\partial_jF_k=\omega_k\omega_j-|\boldsymbol\omega|^2\delta_{kj}
$$
is symmetric in $j,k$. Contracting it with the antisymmetric <Levi-Civita symbol> gives the <curl>
$$
(\nabla\times\mathbf F)_i=\epsilon_{ijk}(\omega_k\omega_j-|\boldsymbol\omega|^2\delta_{kj})=0.
$$
Direct differentiation of the <scalar potential> gives
$$
\partial_i\phi=\omega_i(\boldsymbol\omega\cdot\mathbf x)-|\boldsymbol\omega|^2x_i=F_i,
$$
so **$\mathbf F=\nabla\phi$**. To identify the <level sets>, use the <cross product> identity
$$
\phi=-\tfrac12|\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf x|^2.
$$
If $\boldsymbol\omega\ne0$, write $\mathbf x=\mathbf x_{\parallel}+\mathbf x_{\perp}$ relative to its direction. Then $\phi=-|\boldsymbol\omega|^2|\mathbf x_{\perp}|^2/2$. Thus **each negative level $\phi=c$ is a <circular cylinder> with axis $\mathbb R\boldsymbol\omega$ and radius $\sqrt{-2c}/|\boldsymbol\omega|$**. The zero level is the axis itself; positive levels are empty. If $\boldsymbol\omega=0$, the <scalar potential> is identically zero, so its zero level is all of space and every other level is empty.

= 10C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= Solution
{parent=10C}

Use the right-handed <rotation matrix> which sends the positive $x$ direction to the positive $y$ direction:
$$
R=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The <Cartesian second-rank tensor> transformation law gives
$$
S'=RSR^{\mathsf T}=\begin{pmatrix}
S_{22}&-S_{21}&-S_{23}\\
-S_{12}&S_{11}&S_{13}\\
-S_{32}&S_{31}&S_{33}
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Here <isotropic tensor> means invariant under every proper <rotation>, as is needed for the rank-three assertion involving the <Levi-Civita symbol>. A passive coordinate <rotation> through the same angle uses $R^{\mathsf T}$ instead; the invariance conclusions are identical.

Setting $S'=S$ forces $S_{11}=S_{22}$, $S_{12}=-S_{21}$, and $S_{13}=S_{23}=S_{31}=S_{32}=0$. No symmetry assumption on $S$ has been made: it now has the form
$$
S=\begin{pmatrix}a&b&0\\-b&a&0\\0&0&c\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Invariance under a right-handed quarter-turn about the $x$ axis next gives $S_{22}=S_{33}$ and $S_{12}=-S_{13}$, hence $a=c$ and $b=0$. Conversely $R(\lambda I)R^{\mathsf T}=\lambda I$ for every <orthogonal matrix>. The general <isotropic second-rank tensor> is therefore **$S_{ij}=\lambda\delta_{ij}$**.

An isotropic <vector> must be fixed by the half-turn about the $z$ axis, so its first two components vanish. The half-turn about the $x$ axis then forces its third component to vanish. Thus **the only isotropic <vector> is zero**. The general <isotropic third-rank tensor> under proper <rotations> is **$A_{ijk}=\lambda\epsilon_{ijk}$**. If invariance under the whole <orthogonal group> were required of an ordinary rank-three <tensor>, inversion $R=-I$ would send $A$ to $-A$, forcing $A=0$ instead.

= i
{parent=10c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Both the <Levi-Civita symbol> and $T$ are invariant under proper <rotations>. Their <tensor contraction>
$$
A_l=\epsilon_{ijk}T_{ijkl}
$$
is consequently an isotropic <vector>. The introductory proof shows that it must vanish, so **$\epsilon_{ijk}T_{ijkl}=0$**.

For the displayed combination of <Kronecker deltas>, the three terms are
$$
\alpha\epsilon_{ijk}\delta_{ij}\delta_{kl},\qquad
\beta\epsilon_{ijk}\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl},\qquad
\gamma\epsilon_{ijk}\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}.
$$
In each term the <tensor contraction> repeats two indices of the <Levi-Civita symbol>; each term is zero. This verifies the identity for arbitrary $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$.

= ii
{parent=10c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <tensor contraction> $B_{kl}=\delta_{ij}T_{ijkl}$ is an <isotropic second-rank tensor>, because the <Kronecker delta> and $T$ are invariant under <rotations>. The classification proved above yields **$B_{kl}=\mu\delta_{kl}$** for some scalar $\mu$.

For the given three-term <tensor>, contraction over $i,j$ gives
$$
\delta_{ij}T_{ijkl}=\alpha\delta_{ii}\delta_{kl}+\beta\delta_{kl}+\gamma\delta_{kl}
=\boxed{(3\alpha+\beta+\gamma)\delta_{kl}}.
$$
Thus **$\mu=3\alpha+\beta+\gamma$**.

= iii
{parent=10c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <tensor contraction> $C_{mkl}=\epsilon_{ijm}T_{ijkl}$ is an <isotropic third-rank tensor> under proper <rotations>. It therefore equals $\nu\epsilon_{mkl}$. Cyclically permuting three indices is even, so $\epsilon_{mkl}=\epsilon_{klm}$ and **$\epsilon_{ijm}T_{ijkl}=\nu\epsilon_{klm}$**.

For the given <Kronecker delta> expression, the $\alpha$ term vanishes by antisymmetry, while the $\beta$ and $\gamma$ terms give respectively $\beta\epsilon_{klm}$ and $\gamma\epsilon_{lkm}=-\gamma\epsilon_{klm}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\epsilon_{ijm}T_{ijkl}=(\beta-\gamma)\epsilon_{klm},\qquad \nu=\beta-\gamma.}
$$
These computations also illustrate <isotropic contractions of a fourth-rank tensor>.

= 11C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= a
{parent=11c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <product rule> gives
$$
\nabla\cdot(f\nabla g)=\nabla f\cdot\nabla g+f\nabla^2g.
$$
Apply the <divergence theorem>, with outward <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, and use the vanishing <Laplacian> of the <harmonic function> $g$:
$$
\int_V\nabla f\cdot\nabla g\,dV=\int_S f\nabla g\cdot\mathbf n\,dS.
$$
The boundary value of $f$ is one. Another application of the <divergence theorem> therefore gives
$$
\int_S f\nabla g\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=\int_S\nabla g\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=\int_V\nabla^2g\,dV=\boxed{0}.
$$
This is the <gradient pairing with a boundary-constant function and a harmonic function>, obtained directly from <Green's first identity>. The <harmonic function> must be regular throughout the volume; an interior singularity can supply an additional boundary flux.

= b
{parent=11c}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here $v=z$, so $\nabla v=\mathbf e_z$, while $\nabla u=\widehat{\mathbf r}$ away from the origin. Their <inner product> is $\cos\theta$. The <spherical coordinates> volume element gives
$$
\int_V\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dV=\int_0^a r^2\,dr\int_0^\pi\cos\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta\int_0^{2\pi}d\varphi=\boxed{0}.
$$
This agrees with the boundary-constant <gradient pairing with a boundary-constant function and a harmonic function>: $u$ has constant boundary value $a$ and $v=z$ is a regular <harmonic function>. For comparison with the boundary value one in part (a), use $u/a$ and multiply back by $a$. Strictly, $u=r$ is not differentiable at the origin. Excising a ball of radius $\varepsilon$ resolves this: the inner boundary term $u\partial_n v=-\varepsilon\cos\theta$ has zero angular integral, so it contributes nothing in the limit. Thus the improper <volume integral> has the same zero value.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Now $v=z^2$ and $\nabla v=2z\mathbf e_z$. Since $\nabla u=\widehat{\mathbf r}/a$, their <inner product> is $2r\cos^2\theta/a$. Consequently
$$
\int_V\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dV=\frac2a\int_0^a r^3\,dr\int_0^\pi\cos^2\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta\int_0^{2\pi}d\varphi
=\frac2a\frac{a^4}{4}\frac23(2\pi)=\boxed{\frac{2\pi a^3}{3}}.
$$
Although $u=1$ on the boundary, the <Laplacian> is $\nabla^2v=2$, so $v$ is not a <harmonic function>. There is no contradiction with part (a). The origin singularity in the derivative of $u$ is harmless for this integral: its inner boundary contribution is of order $\varepsilon^4/a$ and tends to zero. More explicitly, <Green's first identity> on the punctured ball gives outer flux $8\pi a^3/3$ and volume term $\int_V2r/a\,dV=2\pi a^3$, whose difference is the displayed answer.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Away from the origin the <gradients> are $\nabla u=\widehat{\mathbf r}/a$ and $\nabla v=-\widehat{\mathbf r}/r^2$. The improper <volume integral> is therefore
$$
\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\int_{\varepsilon<r<a}-\frac{1}{ar^2}\,dV
=-\frac{4\pi}{a}\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\int_\varepsilon^a dr=\boxed{-4\pi}.
$$
The function $v=1/r$ is a <harmonic function> only off the origin. It is singular inside the integration volume, so it does not meet part (a)'s regularity hypothesis. On the punctured ball, <Green's first identity> has outer flux $-4\pi$ and inner flux $4\pi\varepsilon/a$: the inner outward <unit normal> is $-\widehat{\mathbf r}$, so $u\partial_n v=(\varepsilon/a)/\varepsilon^2$. Their sum is $-4\pi+4\pi\varepsilon/a$, exactly the integral before taking the limit. Equivalently the distributional <Laplacian> of $1/r$ is $-4\pi\delta_0$, rather than zero throughout the ball. This is <boundary flux from a singular harmonic potential>; it explains why applying part (a) across the origin would be invalid.

= 12C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= i
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The inequalities $y\le x\le2y$ force $y\ge0$, and the reciprocal bounds exclude zero. Thus the whole region lies in the positive quadrant. Introduce <ratio-product coordinates>
$$
u=\frac{x}{y},\qquad v=xy.
$$
The region becomes the rectangle $1\le u\le2$, $1\le v\le2$. The inverse map and its <Jacobian determinant> are
$$
x=\sqrt{uv},\qquad y=\sqrt{v/u},\qquad
\frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}=\begin{vmatrix}x/(2u)&x/(2v)\\-y/(2u)&y/(2v)\end{vmatrix}=\frac{xy}{2uv}=\frac1{2u}>0.
$$
The <change of variables formula> then gives
$$
\int_A\frac{x}{y}\,dx\,dy=\int_1^2\int_1^2u\frac1{2u}\,dv\,du=\boxed{\frac12}.
$$

= ii
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take the usual positive, counterclockwise <boundary orientation>; the printed question does not specify one, and reversing it negates the answer. Write the successive corner points as $P=(1,1)$, $Q=(\sqrt2,1/\sqrt2)$, $R=(2,1)$, $S=(\sqrt2,\sqrt2)$. Traverse $P\to Q\to R\to S\to P$, keeping the region on the left.

\Image[paper-3-region.png]
{title=Counterclockwise boundary of the region between two rays and two hyperbolas}

On $PQ$, use $y=1/x$ with $1\le x\le\sqrt2$. Here $dy=-dx/x^2$, so the <line integral> is
$$
I_{PQ}=\int_1^{\sqrt2}\left(-\frac{x}{2}-1\right)dx=\frac34-\sqrt2.
$$
On $QR$, use $y=x/2$ with $\sqrt2\le x\le2$:
$$
I_{QR}=\int_{\sqrt2}^{2}\left(\frac{x}{2}-1\right)dx=\sqrt2-\frac32.
$$
On $RS$, use $y=2/x$ with $x$ decreasing from $2$ to $\sqrt2$:
$$
I_{RS}=\int_2^{\sqrt2}\left(-\frac{x}{2}-1\right)dx=\frac52-\sqrt2.
$$
Finally, on $SP$, use $y=x$ with $x$ decreasing from $\sqrt2$ to $1$:
$$
I_{SP}=\int_{\sqrt2}^{1}\left(\frac{x}{2}-1\right)dx=\sqrt2-\frac54.
$$
Adding all four <line integrals> gives **$\oint_C(x^2/(2y))\,dy-dx=1/2$** for the positive orientation, or **$-1/2$** for the reverse orientation.

= iii
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The equality with part (i) follows from <Green's theorem>. For the coefficients $P=-1$ and $Q=x^2/(2y)$,
$$
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}=\frac{x}{y}.
$$
They are smooth on a neighborhood of this region, since $y>0$. Thus for the positively oriented boundary,
$$
\boxed{\oint_C P\,dx+Q\,dy=\int_A\frac{x}{y}\,dx\,dy=\frac12.}
$$
The term $-dx$ itself has zero <line integral> around the closed <curve>, although it contributes to the four individual edge integrals.
